/-!
# Bilinear smoothing along distinct monomials

This file proves the **bilinear smoothing inequality along distinct monomials**
(`Auto.monomial_smoothing`): let `α₁ ≠ α₂` be positive integers and `0 < a < b`. There are
`C < ∞` and `δ > 0` such that for every `ψ ∈ C¹(ℝ)` supported in `[a, b]`, every `λ ≥ 1` and all
Schwartz functions `g₁, g₂` on `ℝ²` with `supp ĝ_j ⊆ {|ξ_j| ≥ λ}` for at least one `j`,
the bilinear average `A_ψ(g₁, g₂)(x) = ∫ ψ(t) g₁(x + t^{α₁} e₁) g₂(x + t^{α₂} e₂) dt` satisfies
`‖A_ψ(g₁, g₂)‖₁ ≤ C (‖ψ‖_∞ + ‖ψ'‖₁) λ^{-δ} ‖g₁‖₂ ‖g₂‖₂`, with `δ` independent of `ψ`.

The statement uses only Mathlib notions (Schwartz functions on `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)`, the
Fourier transform `𝓕`, `eLpNorm`) and the local definition `Auto.ms8Average` of `A_ψ`.

The proof follows the blueprint `blueprints/task_1_smoothing_inequality_2.tex`, which implements the
Christ-Durcik-Roos method with a modified flow:
the frequency decomposition into sharp and flat parts (Section 2), the sublevel estimate for the
modified flow (Section 3), an `L^p`-improving bound (Section 4), the local correlation estimate
(Lemma 5.1), local smoothing for bounded frequency-localized inputs (Proposition 6.1), global `L²`
smoothing (Section 7) and the reduction to `C¹` cutoffs and monomials (Section 8). Deviations from
the blueprint are recorded in `automation/ErrorReport-task1.md`.

## References

* M. Christ, P. Durcik, J. Roos, *Trilinear smoothing inequalities and a variant of the triangular
  Hilbert transform*, [arXiv:2008.10140](https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.10140).
-/

